{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And did not keep it.</b> It may be explained following what they said in chapter four and five (Bava Kamma 45a): Rabbi Eliezer says, the only guarding required is with a knife. Rabba said: What is the reason? For the verse says ״וְלֹא יִשְׁמְרֶנּוּ״ — again there is no guarding required for this one. Abbaye said to him: If so, ״וְלֹא יְכַסֶּנּוּ״ too, again there is none required for him, etc., and so forth. And it appears that Rabba's inference stands firm, for there is a difference between ״וְלֹא יְכַסֶּנּוּ״ — where the verse did not change the register of speech it began with, ״כִּי יִפְתַּח״ in the future tense, ״כִּי יִכְרֶה״ etc., ״וְלֹא יְכַסֶּנּוּ״ in the future tense — unlike ״וְלֹא יִשְׁמְרֶנּוּ״, where the verse began speaking in the past tense, ״אוֹ נוֹדַע״ etc., and concluded speaking in the future tense, ״וְלֹא יִשְׁמְרֶנּוּ״ — when it should have said ״וְלֹא שְׁמָרוֹ״ and that would have been correct. And there Rabba did not trouble to answer Abbaye; and according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, who exempts him with any guarding, even a lesser one, the verse said in this order ״וְלֹא יִשְׁמְרֶנּוּ״ to derive this teaching and not the other; and see there at the end of the chapter." "<b>Shall pay ox.</b> Perhaps, that even though they said (Bava Kamma 5a) that an established gorer pays from the best of his property, if the owner of the damaging animal had property better than this, he can discharge his obligation with equivalent value to his ox — and they said 'best property' only to exclude less than this."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase וְקָאֵי וְקָאֵי (literally 'stands and stands') is rendered idiomatically as 'stands firm'; the expression וְכוּ׳ עַד כָּאן is rendered 'etc., and so forth' to mark the Talmudic ellipsis point."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:20.386299922Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}